# Benchmark requirement for each delivered stage

Martin added this requirement during Phase C: every delivered stage must
include a revision-bound comparison against the established curves and v1.

Signature comparison: Ed25519, Ed448, Ed301-EdDSA-v1, Ed301-EdDSA-v2.
DH comparison: X25519, X448, X301-v1, X301-v2.

Martin subsequently clarified: benchmark what is available at each stage;
do not pull Phase-D implementation work into Phase C for the benchmark.
In particular, the X301-v2 Rust/integration measurements follow in Phase D.
An unavailable lane is a recorded stage boundary, not permission to change
the implementation order or invent a timing result.

The requirement supplements point 8 of the assignment; it does not authorize
different API layers to be combined into one ranking. Measure core, EVP/
provider and later format/TLS paths separately, on the same machine with the
same toolchain/profile where applicable. Report repetitions, dispersion,
input sizes, setup/caching boundaries, source and binary hashes, and gaps.

If an implementation/API lane does not exist yet, explicitly mark it as
unavailable. Never substitute Python-reference timings for a compiled v2
implementation or compare Rust-core calls against EVP calls as one ranking.
Do not transfer old measurements to new sources or binaries. Numeric regression
thresholds have not been assigned; report observed differences without inventing
a 3-percent release gate or treating timing-leak tests as speed benchmarks.

Common-signature comparisons use empty contexts where all four profiles have
the same operation shape. Ed301-specific binary/255-byte contexts are measured
separately. Cold key expansion/import and prepared-key operations must remain
distinguishable. DH derivation is not an authenticated TLS handshake.
